 "findings": [
  {
   "evidence": "Both steps of the proof are iffs: `isArithFrobAt_iff_galEquivZMod_eq_absNorm` (Cyclotomic/Frobenius.lean:182) and `Ideal.isArithFrobAt_ringOfIntegers_rat_iff` (NumberField/Ideal/IntegersRat.lean:121); the only other ingredient, `hnorm`, is an equation. So the reverse direction needs no extra hypothesis or instance \u2014 the parent file derives `IsGalois`/`Finite` internally (Frobenius.lean:164-166).",
   "file": "TauCeti/NumberTheory/NumberField/Cyclotomic/ArithmeticFrobenius.lean",
   "fix": "State it as `isArithFrobAt_iff_galEquivZMod_eq_unitOfCoprime : IsArithFrobAt \u2124 \u03c3 Q \u2194 Rat.galEquivZMod n K \u03c3 = ZMod.unitOfCoprime p hp`, reusing the same `hnorm`/`v`/`hn` for both directions; the existing binders (including `[Q.IsPrime]`) already suffice, and `galEquivZMod_arithFrobAt` then follows from `.mp`.",
   "issue": "`galEquivZMod_eq_unitOfCoprime_of_isArithFrobAt` states only `IsArithFrobAt \u2124 \u03c3 Q \u2192 Rat.galEquivZMod n K \u03c3 = ZMod.unitOfCoprime p hp`. A consumer that knows the cyclotomic exponent cannot conclude \u03c3 is the Frobenius at Q, and recovering that requires re-deriving the `absNorm (Q.under (\ud835\udcde \u211a)) = p` computation and the `HeightOneSpectrum` packaging that this proof hides.",
   "line": "41"
  },
  {
   "evidence": "Each proof is `galEquivZMod_eq_unitOfCoprime_of_isArithFrobAt Q (by decide) h\u03c3` plus a `decide`/`Units.ext` step (lines 41-43, 49-51, 58-60, 66-68, 74-77), all of which are reversible; the existing consumer `frobeniusVonMangoldtCoeff_cyclotomic_five_of_galEquivZMod_eq_two` (Chebotarev/PrimeCounting/ArithmeticProgression.lean:166) takes the character value as input, i.e. travels in the direction the file does not provide.",
   "file": "TauCeti/NumberTheory/NumberField/WorkedExamples/FifthCyclotomic/Frobenius.lean",
   "fix": "Once the general lemma is an iff, restate these as iffs: `IsArithFrobAt \u2124 \u03c3 Q \u2194 (Rat.galEquivZMod 5 K \u03c3 : ZMod 5) = 2` (and 3, 2, -1), and `IsArithFrobAt \u2124 \u03c3 Q \u2194 \u03c3 = 1` at 11.",
   "issue": "All five worked-example statements inherit the one-way shape: lines 38, 46, 54, 63, 71. Most consequentially, `eq_one_of_isArithFrobAt_eleven` gives only `IsArithFrobAt \u2124 \u03c3 Q \u2192 \u03c3 = 1`; its converse \u2014 the identity *is* an arithmetic Frobenius at a prime over 11, i.e. 11 splits completely \u2014 is the statement a consumer wants and is not derivable from the forward implication.",
   "line": "38"
  }
 ],
